  {
   "id": "wwtsYPiMTTkJ",
   "from": "Leon-Alph",
   "author": "33efe9163118191a",
   "date": "2014-07-18T17:52:50Z",
   "subject": "p-value gaussian linear",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hi,</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">\nI&#39;ve trouble concerning the possibility to compute a Bayesian p_value in the\nRstan code.<br>\nin a previous thread (Simple latent Poisson time series model: Sampling versus\nOptimizing), i saw this code:<br></span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;background:#fafafa\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">yrep</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">~</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#660066\">Poisson</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#000088\">lambda</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]);</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"></span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">and then computing the p_value as:</span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;background:#fafafa\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">b</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">.</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">pvalue </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">~</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">yrep</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">&gt;</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> y</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">])</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">-</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#006666\">0.5</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">*</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">   </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">yrep</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">==</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> y</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]);</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"></span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">which seems particularly easy... but\nI don&#39;t understand where y[i] come from.<br>\n<br>\nIf I use the following model:</span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;background:#fafafa\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">y</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">~</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#660066\">Normal</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(mu[</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:black\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">],sigma);</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"></span></p>\n\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><br>\nI have no idea how to compute a yrep[i] . I saw how to assess a p-value after simulation, but I would like to do it\ndireclty in the code.<br>\n<br>\nCould someone give an example on how to compute a Bayesian p-value with a simple linear\ngaussian regression ?<br>\n<br>\nThank you</span></p>\n\n</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "MPLtZTKzlm8J",
   "from": "Andrew Gelman",
   "author": "da33143a8db60ce8",
   "date": "2014-07-18T22:35:15Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] p-value gaussian linear",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">The y[i]’s are the data in the model.  The y_rep[i]’s need to be defined in the generated quantities block using a random number generation call such as:<div>y[i] ~ normal_rng(mu[i],sigma);</div><div><br></div><div>Once you’ve done it, though, I recommend doing a graphical test rather than a p-value (see chapter 6 of BDA for more discussion of this issue).</div><div><br></div><div>A</div><div><br><div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><div>On Jul 18, 2014, at 7:52 PM, Leon-Alph &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">leon.cor...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">Hi,</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:12.0pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">\r\nI&#39;ve trouble concerning the possibility to compute a Bayesian p_value in the\r\nRstan code.<br>\r\nin a previous thread (Simple latent Poisson time series model: Sampling versus\r\nOptimizing), i saw this code:<br></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;background:#fafafa\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">yrep</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">~</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#660066\">Poisson</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#000088\">lambda</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]);</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">and then computing the p_value as:</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;background:#fafafa\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">b</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">.</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">pvalue </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">~</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">yrep</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">&gt;</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> y</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">])</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">-</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#006666\">0.5</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">*</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">   </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">yrep</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">==</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> y</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]);</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\">which seems particularly easy... but\r\nI don&#39;t understand where y[i] come from.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIf I use the following model:</span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0cm;margin-bottom:.0001pt;line-height:normal;background:#fafafa\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">y</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">]</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">~</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#660066\">Normal</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">(mu[</span><span style=\"font-size:10pt;font-family:&#39;Courier New&#39;\">i</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;color:#666600\">],sigma);</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;\"></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:12.0pt;line-height:115%;font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,&quot;serif&quot;\"><br>\r\nI have no idea how to compute a yrep[i] . I saw how to assess a p-value after simulation, but I would like to do it\r\ndireclty in the code.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nCould someone give an example on how to compute a Bayesian p-value with a simple linear\r\ngaussian regression ?<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThank you</span></p>\r\n\r\n</div><div><br></div></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\">\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;Stan users mailing list&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">stan-users+...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792466242761000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1WIOnUcvmMOTePtRfvgJWu\">https://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</blockquote></div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "-o36d-XfTEsJ",
   "from": "Christian Roy",
   "author": "54e08ddecb38c1b3",
   "date": "2014-07-19T01:52:05Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] p-value gaussian linear",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\">I think that If you want to use the random number generator in the generated quantities block you will need to use the arrow instead of the tilde.</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"gmail_extra\">A simple model should look something like:</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\">data {</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  int&lt;lower=0&gt; N;   // number of Observations</div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"gmail_extra\">  real y[N];             //  Observed data<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  real x[N];            //  Explanatory variables</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"> </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">}</div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"gmail_extra\">parameters {</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  real alpha; </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  real beta; </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma; </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">}</div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"gmail_extra\">transformed parameters {</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  real mu;   </div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  mu &lt;- alpha + beta * x;</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">}</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">model {</div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\"gmail_extra\">  alpha ~ normal(0,10);</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  beta ~ normal(0,10);</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">  sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">\r\n  //Likelihood</div>\r\n<div class=\"gmail_extra\">  y ~ normal(mu, sigma);</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">}</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">generated quantities{</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"> real yrep[N]</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">\r\n\r\n //predicted values<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"> yrep &lt;- normal_rng(mu,sigma);</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">}</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">\r\n\r\nChris</div><div class=\"gmail_extra\">-----------------</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Jul 18, 2014 at 6:35 PM, Andrew Gelman <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gel...@stat.columbia.edu</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">y[i] ~ normal_rng(mu[i],sigma);</blockquote></div><br><br>\r\n\r\n</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "un5uD0T0TYMJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2014-07-19T05:19:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] p-value gaussian linear",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Christian&#39;s right --- you need to use the left-arrow.  \r<br>\r<br>The backstory is that we thought long and hard about whether\r<br>to interpret\r<br>\r<br>   y ~ normal(0,1);\r<br>\r<br>as\r<br>\r<br>  y &lt;- normal_rng(0,1);\r<br>\r<br>in the generated quantities block.  Don&#39;t know if we ever\r<br>came to full agreement, but we went with the latter.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On Jul 18, 2014, at 9:51 PM, Christian Roy &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">christia...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>\r<br>&gt; I think that If you want to use the random number generator in the generated quantities block you will need to use the arrow instead of the tilde.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; A simple model should look something like:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; data {\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=0&gt; N;   // number of Observations\r<br>&gt;   real y[N];             //  Observed data\r<br>&gt;   real x[N];            //  Explanatory variables\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; parameters {\r<br>&gt;   real alpha; \r<br>&gt;   real beta; \r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower=0&gt; sigma; \r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; transformed parameters {\r<br>&gt;   real mu;   \r<br>&gt;   mu &lt;- alpha + beta * x;\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; model {\r<br>&gt;   alpha ~ normal(0,10);\r<br>&gt;   beta ~ normal(0,10);\r<br>&gt;   sigma ~ cauchy(0,5);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   //Likelihood\r<br>&gt;   y ~ normal(mu, sigma);\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; generated quantities{\r<br>&gt;  real yrep[N]\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;  //predicted values\r<br>&gt;  yrep &lt;- normal_rng(mu,sigma);\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Chris\r<br>&gt; -----------------\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; On Fri, Jul 18, 2014 at 6:35 PM, Andrew Gelman &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gel...@stat.columbia.edu</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; y[i] ~ normal_rng(mu[i],sigma);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
